# Streaming, seen as it happens: a real reply with headings, a list and a
# code block, shot about once a second while it streams. Read the shots for
# the busy line, the caret, raw markdown settling into the rendered block,
# and anything that jumps or flickers. No file changes; no tools needed.
@real
@home scratch
@size 120x40
@setup printf 'package main\n\nfunc main() {}\n' > main.go && git add -A && git -c user.name=qa -c user.email=qa@example.invalid commit -qm main
WAITFOR /describe a task/ 30
SEND Without using any tools, explain Go's context package in about 200 words: a short heading, a bulleted list of three uses, and one small code example.
WAITFOR /esc to stop/ 30
SHOT stream-01
WAIT 0.6
SHOT stream-02
WAIT 0.6
SHOT stream-03
WAIT 0.6
SHOT stream-04
WAIT 0.6
SHOT stream-05
WAIT 0.6
SHOT stream-06
WAIT 0.6
SHOT stream-07
WAIT 0.6
SHOT stream-08
WAIT 0.6
SHOT stream-09
WAIT 0.6
SHOT stream-10
WAIT 0.6
SHOT stream-11
WAIT 0.6
SHOT stream-12
WAIT 0.6
SHOT stream-13
WAIT 0.6
SHOT stream-14
WAIT 0.6
IDLE 300
SHOT done
EXPECT /context/
EXPECT_NOT /panic: |goroutine [0-9]+ \[/
